{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_014w8yNyt6dFnj7t7Qhex1EM","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"{"commits":[{"hash":"0803b6ebda4a7acf26659df193707d8ad8d68c61","date":"2026-10-02T12:59:36+03:00","message":"(fix) O3-6026: Prefetch the vitals charts and keep their height while they load (#3727)","refs":"","body":"* (fix) Prefetch the vitals charts and keep their height while they load\n\nThe vitals and biometrics widgets now start loading their chart code when\nsomeone hovers over or focuses the table/chart switcher. By the time they\nclick, the chart chunk is usually ready and the chart renders without a\nloading state.\n\nWhen the chunk isn't ready yet, the fallback is now a SkeletonPlaceholder\nthe same height as the rendered chart view (466px plus margins) instead of\na one-line InlineLoading. The spinner shrank the card to a single line and\npulled the widgets below it up, then pushed them back down when the chart\nrendered. The skeleton keeps the card at its final height. It keeps\nrole=\"progressbar\" and a \"Loading\" label for screen readers.\n\n* Ignore failed chart prefetches\n\nThe hover and focus handlers returned the import promise, so a failed\nchunk download on hover became an unhandled rejection, and O3's global\nhandler showed an error toast before anyone opened the chart view.\n\nThe handlers now call a prefetch wrapper that ignores the failure.\nReact.lazy still uses the uncaught loader, so opening the chart view\nretries the download, and a failure there still reaches an error\nboundary.\n\n* Test the chart prefetch in the vitals and biometrics widgets\n\nEach widget gets two tests: hovering the table/chart switcher starts\nloading the chart module, and a failed prefetch leaves the table in\nplace without an unhandled rejection. Vitest fails the run on an\nunhandled rejection, so removing the prefetch's catch fails the suite.\n\nThe tests mock the chart module with vi.doMock and import a fresh copy\nof the widget after vi.resetModules, so each test starts with its own\nReact.lazy state and a chart module that hasn't been imported yet.\n\n---------","authorName":"Dennis Kigen","authorEmail":"kigen.work@gmail.com"},{"hash":"54e48e8f97ee116b97829f2b9df66c699d27dd4d","date":"2026-09-29T10:07:27+03:00","message":"(fix) Stop loading code the patient chart summary doesn't use (#3712)","refs":"","body":"* (fix) Stop loading code the patient chart summary doesn't use\n\nRegister the growth chart dashboard page with getAsyncLifecycle, so it\nonly loads when someone opens the growth chart.\n\nIt was registered with getSyncLifecycle, which put the page and\neverything it imports into the code the module loads as soon as it\nstarts. Rendering the Growth chart link in the left nav starts the\nmodule, so every patient chart load also downloaded Carbon Charts and\n338 KiB of chart CSS. The module now loads 2 KiB up front instead of\n533 KiB.\n\n* Lazy-load the vitals and biometrics charts\n\nLoad the vitals and biometrics chart components with React.lazy when\nsomeone switches to the chart view, showing the same inline loading\nindicator as the vitals header while they load.\n\nBoth widgets show a table by default, but they imported their chart\ncomponents directly, so @carbon/charts-react loaded with the patient\nsummary whether or not anyone opened a chart. The chart view tests now\nwait for the chart to load. The vitals module now loads 207 KiB up front\ninstead of 559 KiB.\n\n* Register the procedures form workspace asynchronously\n\nRegister the procedures form workspace with getAsyncLifecycle, like the\nother 20 workspaces in this repo.\n\nWith getSyncLifecycle, zod and react-hook-form loaded as soon as the\nProcedures link rendered in the left nav. The procedures module now\nloads 18 KiB up front instead of 121 KiB.\n\n* Don't share lodash-es from the patient chart app\n\nRemove lodash-es from esm-patient-chart-app's peerDependencies and keep\nit as a regular dependency, so the build only bundles the functions the\napp imports.\n\nListing it as a peer dependency made it a shared module. The app shell\ndoesn't provide lodash-es, so the chart app always loaded its own copy,\nand the build can't drop unused code from a shared module, so that copy\nwas the whole package (77 KiB) for the three functions the app uses.\n\n---------","authorName":"Dennis Kigen","authorEmail":"kigen.work@gmail.com"},{"hash":"03e6d382ce8bc5d0723dc2d4b7217d410f9b3c2f","date":"2026-08-20T17:32:53+05:30","message":"(fix) O3-5841: Sort Vitals and Biometrics tables in the direction the header arrow indicates (#3525)","refs":"","body":"* (fix) O3-5841: Sort Vitals and Biometrics tables in the direction the header arrow indicates\n\nPaginatedVitals and PaginatedBiometrics bypass Carbon's row sorting and\nre-sort the full dataset themselves so that sorting spans pages. Two things\nwere wrong with that.\n\nThe direction mapping was inverted: each column's ascending comparator was\nused as-is for DESC and negated for ASC. Carbon treats ASC as ascending and\nonly rotates the header's ArrowUp icon for DESC, so an ascending arrow put\nthe newest reading at the top and a descending arrow put the oldest one\nthere. Negate for DESC instead, which lines the rendered order up with both\nthe arrow and the header's aria-sort value for every sortable column in\nthese tables.\n\nSorting also never settled. handleSorting stored a new sortParams object on\nevery call, and Carbon does not treat sortRow as a pure comparator -\ngetDerivedStateFromProps calls it again whenever sortHeaderKey is set. The\nnew object changed the sortedData reference, the new rows reference\nre-triggered Carbon's rows effect, that effect derived state again, and the\ncycle repeated until the heap was exhausted. Before the first click\nsortHeaderKey is null, so the loop only started once a header was clicked.\n\nbiometrics-overview.test already declared the expected ascending and\ndescending orders but never asserted them, which is how the inversion went\nunnoticed; wire those assertions up and add equivalent coverage for the\nvitals table, which had none.\n\n* (fix) O3-5841: Capture sort state from the header click instead of sortRow\n\nCarbon skips `sortRow` entirely when a header transitions to `NONE`, and never\ninvokes it when the page holds a single row, because `Array.prototype.sort`\nperforms no comparisons on a one-element array. In both cases `sortParams` kept\nits previous value while the header state moved on, so `aria-sort` and the\nrendered order disagreed.\n\nRead the next sort state from `getHeaderProps`'s `onClick` callback, which Carbon\ncalls exactly once per click with the state it is transitioning to, and leave\n`sortRow` as a pure `() => 0`. That covers all three sort states.\n\nAdds coverage for both cases in the Vitals and Biometrics tables.\n\n* (fix) O3-5841: Extract the shared table sorting into a hook\n\nThe sorting was identical line for line in PaginatedVitals and\nPaginatedBiometrics, so a future change would have had to be made in both\nfiles. Move the sort state, the header click handler and the full-dataset\nre-sort into a `useTableSorting` hook in the package's `common` folder, and\nexport the inert comparator alongside it as `noopSortRow`.\n\nThe hook types its sort direction with Carbon's `DataTableSortState` rather\nthan restating 'ASC' | 'DESC' | 'NONE' by hand, which is what the click\nhandler was already using.\n\nAlso give the biometrics column headers an explicit `key={header.key}`\ninstead of letting it arrive through the `getHeaderProps` spread, matching\nPaginatedVitals and avoiding React 19's warning about keys in spreads, and\ndefine `getRowDates` once per test file instead of once per test.\n\n* Spread vitals config object mock","authorName":"Sathieskumar Sudeshkar","authorEmail":"sudeshkar008sk@gmail.com"},{"hash":"09db7da4c93818faf08a8db1f87e191e465de3eb","date":"2026-08-12T11:22:48+03:00","message":"(chore) Adopt @openmrs/eslint-config and ESLint 9 (#3564)","refs":"","body":"","authorName":"Dennis Kigen","authorEmail":"kigen.work@gmail.com"},{"hash":"bda2ce4aac69061ef4db6b45c88455f8cbd30e4c","date":"2026-05-13T19:56:26+03:00","message":"(chore) Migrate from jest to vitest (#3301)","refs":"","body":"","authorName":"Dennis Kigen","authorEmail":"kigen.work@gmail.com"},{"hash":"d4857fbf84af21943b989c91c1b37277ff3ff2fd","date":"2026-04-17T16:01:13+03:00","message":"(feat) Surface BMI reference ranges in the biometrics table (#3238)","refs":"","body":"","authorName":"Dennis Kigen","authorEmail":"kigen.work@gmail.com"},{"hash":"98e9eed004265810e4d86f3d6de873161307ba65","date":"2026-04-10T21:58:34+03:00","message":"(fix) O3-5599: Use clean, lowercase, hyphenated URL paths for dashboard slugs (#3217)","refs":"","body":"","authorName":"Dennis Kigen","authorEmail":"kigen.work@gmail.com"}],"totalCount":7}"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"35734605-67c4-4533-9a89-ce661590928e","uuid":"16598036-2dd5-49f7-85de-bab2cb3595c2","timestamp":"2026-10-23T18:06:25.736Z","tool_use_result":[{"type":"text","text":"{"commits":[{"hash":"0803b6ebda4a7acf26659df193707d8ad8d68c61","date":"2026-10-02T12:59:36+03:00","message":"(fix) O3-6026: Prefetch the vitals charts and keep their height while they load (#3727)","refs":"","body":"* (fix) Prefetch the vitals charts and keep their height while they load\n\nThe vitals and biometrics widgets now start loading their chart code when\nsomeone hovers over or focuses the table/chart switcher. By the time they\nclick, the chart chunk is usually ready and the chart renders without a\nloading state.\n\nWhen the chunk isn't ready yet, the fallback is now a SkeletonPlaceholder\nthe same height as the rendered chart view (466px plus margins) instead of\na one-line InlineLoading. The spinner shrank the card to a single line and\npulled the widgets below it up, then pushed them back down when the chart\nrendered. The skeleton keeps the card at its final height. It keeps\nrole=\"progressbar\" and a \"Loading\" label for screen readers.\n\n* Ignore failed chart prefetches\n\nThe hover and focus handlers returned the import promise, so a failed\nchunk download on hover became an unhandled rejection, and O3's global\nhandler showed an error toast before anyone opened the chart view.\n\nThe handlers now call a prefetch wrapper that ignores the failure.\nReact.lazy still uses the uncaught loader, so opening the chart view\nretries the download, and a failure there still reaches an error\nboundary.\n\n* Test the chart prefetch in the vitals and biometrics widgets\n\nEach widget gets two tests: hovering the table/chart switcher starts\nloading the chart module, and a failed prefetch leaves the table in\nplace without an unhandled rejection. Vitest fails the run on an\nunhandled rejection, so removing the prefetch's catch fails the suite.\n\nThe tests mock the chart module with vi.doMock and import a fresh copy\nof the widget after vi.resetModules, so each test starts with its own\nReact.lazy state and a chart module that hasn't been imported yet.\n\n---------","authorName":"Dennis Kigen","authorEmail":"kigen.work@gmail.com"},{"hash":"54e48e8f97ee116b97829f2b9df66c699d27dd4d","date":"2026-09-29T10:07:27+03:00","message":"(fix) Stop loading code the patient chart summary doesn't use (#3712)","refs":"","body":"* (fix) Stop loading code the patient chart summary doesn't use\n\nRegister the growth chart dashboard page with getAsyncLifecycle, so it\nonly loads when someone opens the growth chart.\n\nIt was registered with getSyncLifecycle, which put the page and\neverything it imports into the code the module loads as soon as it\nstarts. Rendering the Growth chart link in the left nav starts the\nmodule, so every patient chart load also downloaded Carbon Charts and\n338 KiB of chart CSS. The module now loads 2 KiB up front instead of\n533 KiB.\n\n* Lazy-load the vitals and biometrics charts\n\nLoad the vitals and biometrics chart components with React.lazy when\nsomeone switches to the chart view, showing the same inline loading\nindicator as the vitals header while they load.\n\nBoth widgets show a table by default, but they imported their chart\ncomponents directly, so @carbon/charts-react loaded with the patient\nsummary whether or not anyone opened a chart. The chart view tests now\nwait for the chart to load. The vitals module now loads 207 KiB up front\ninstead of 559 KiB.\n\n* Register the procedures form workspace asynchronously\n\nRegister the procedures form workspace with getAsyncLifecycle, like the\nother 20 workspaces in this repo.\n\nWith getSyncLifecycle, zod and react-hook-form loaded as soon as the\nProcedures link rendered in the left nav. The procedures module now\nloads 18 KiB up front instead of 121 KiB.\n\n* Don't share lodash-es from the patient chart app\n\nRemove lodash-es from esm-patient-chart-app's peerDependencies and keep\nit as a regular dependency, so the build only bundles the functions the\napp imports.\n\nListing it as a peer dependency made it a shared module. The app shell\ndoesn't provide lodash-es, so the chart app always loaded its own copy,\nand the build can't drop unused code from a shared module, so that copy\nwas the whole package (77 KiB) for the three functions the app uses.\n\n---------","authorName":"Dennis Kigen","authorEmail":"kigen.work@gmail.com"},{"hash":"03e6d382ce8bc5d0723dc2d4b7217d410f9b3c2f","date":"2026-08-20T17:32:53+05:30","message":"(fix) O3-5841: Sort Vitals and Biometrics tables in the direction the header arrow indicates (#3525)","refs":"","body":"* (fix) O3-5841: Sort Vitals and Biometrics tables in the direction the header arrow indicates\n\nPaginatedVitals and PaginatedBiometrics bypass Carbon's row sorting and\nre-sort the full dataset themselves so that sorting spans pages. Two things\nwere wrong with that.\n\nThe direction mapping was inverted: each column's ascending comparator was\nused as-is for DESC and negated for ASC. Carbon treats ASC as ascending and\nonly rotates the header's ArrowUp icon for DESC, so an ascending arrow put\nthe newest reading at the top and a descending arrow put the oldest one\nthere. Negate for DESC instead, which lines the rendered order up with both\nthe arrow and the header's aria-sort value for every sortable column in\nthese tables.\n\nSorting also never settled. handleSorting stored a new sortParams object on\nevery call, and Carbon does not treat sortRow as a pure comparator -\ngetDerivedStateFromProps calls it again whenever sortHeaderKey is set. The\nnew object changed the sortedData reference, the new rows reference\nre-triggered Carbon's rows effect, that effect derived state again, and the\ncycle repeated until the heap was exhausted. Before the first click\nsortHeaderKey is null, so the loop only started once a header was clicked.\n\nbiometrics-overview.test already declared the expected ascending and\ndescending orders but never asserted them, which is how the inversion went\nunnoticed; wire those assertions up and add equivalent coverage for the\nvitals table, which had none.\n\n* (fix) O3-5841: Capture sort state from the header click instead of sortRow\n\nCarbon skips `sortRow` entirely when a header transitions to `NONE`, and never\ninvokes it when the page holds a single row, because `Array.prototype.sort`\nperforms no comparisons on a one-element array. In both cases `sortParams` kept\nits previous value while the header state moved on, so `aria-sort` and the\nrendered order disagreed.\n\nRead the next sort state from `getHeaderProps`'s `onClick` callback, which Carbon\ncalls exactly once per click with the state it is transitioning to, and leave\n`sortRow` as a pure `() => 0`. That covers all three sort states.\n\nAdds coverage for both cases in the Vitals and Biometrics tables.\n\n* (fix) O3-5841: Extract the shared table sorting into a hook\n\nThe sorting was identical line for line in PaginatedVitals and\nPaginatedBiometrics,